import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Discrete math is the math of computer science. It starts with logic: the rules of valid reasoning.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A proposition is a statement that is either true or false. Logical connectives combine them: ¬ (not), ∧ (and), ∨ (or), → (if-then), ↔ (if and only if)."},{"t":"key","text":"p → q is false only when p is true and q is false. Its contrapositive ¬q → ¬p is logically equivalent; its converse q → p is not."},{"t":"p","text":"Truth tables list every combination of truth values. Two statements are logically equivalent if their truth tables match. De Morgan's laws: ¬(p ∧ q) ≡ ¬p ∨ ¬q and ¬(p ∨ q) ≡ ¬p ∧ ¬q."},{"t":"ex","problem":"When is \"If it rains, then the game is canceled\" false?","steps":["An implication is false only when the hypothesis is true and the conclusion is false"],"answer":"When it rains and the game is NOT canceled"}]},{"say":"Mathematicians prove things with airtight logic. Let's learn the main proof strategies.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Quantifiers: ∀ (\"for all\") and ∃ (\"there exists\"). Negating swaps them: ¬∀x P(x) ≡ ∃x ¬P(x)."},{"t":"key","text":"Proof techniques: direct proof (assume p, show q), proof by contrapositive (assume ¬q, show ¬p), proof by contradiction (assume the statement is false and derive a contradiction), and mathematical induction."},{"t":"p","text":"Induction proves a statement for all n ≥ 1: prove the base case P(1), then show P(k) → P(k + 1)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Prove the sum of two even numbers is even.","steps":["Let a = 2m and b = 2n for integers m, n","a + b = 2m + 2n = 2(m + n)"],"answer":"a + b is 2 times an integer, so it's even"},{"t":"p","text":"A single counterexample disproves a \"for all\" statement."}]}],[{"say":"Sets are collections of objects. They're the language for describing everything in discrete math.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Set operations: union A ∪ B (in either), intersection A ∩ B (in both), difference A − B, and complement. |A| is the number of elements. The power set of A has 2^|A| subsets."},{"t":"key","text":"Inclusion–exclusion: |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B|."},{"t":"p","text":"A function f: A → B assigns each element of A exactly one element of B."},{"t":"list","items":["Injective (one-to-one): different inputs give different outputs.","Surjective (onto): every element of B is hit.","Bijective: both, so the function has an inverse."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"30 students take math, 20 take art, 8 take both. How many take at least one?","steps":["30 + 20 − 8"],"answer":"42"}]},{"say":"Relations describe connections between elements, like \"is less than\" or \"is friends with.\"","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A relation on a set A is a set of ordered pairs from A. Properties:"},{"t":"list","items":["Reflexive: every element relates to itself (a R a).","Symmetric: a R b implies b R a.","Antisymmetric: a R b and b R a imply a = b.","Transitive: a R b and b R c imply a R c."]},{"t":"key","text":"An equivalence relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive; it splits a set into equivalence classes. A partial order is reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Is \"has the same birthday as\" an equivalence relation?","steps":["Reflexive, symmetric and transitive all hold"],"answer":"Yes; the equivalence classes are the groups sharing a birthday"}]}],[{"say":"Number theory studies whole numbers. It powers modern cryptography.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"a divides b (written a | b) if b = ak for some integer k. A prime has exactly two positive divisors: 1 and itself."},{"t":"key","text":"Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic: every integer greater than 1 has a unique prime factorization."},{"t":"p","text":"The Euclidean algorithm finds the GCD quickly: gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b), repeating until the remainder is 0."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find gcd(84, 36).","steps":["84 = 2·36 + 12, so gcd(36, 12)","36 = 3·12 + 0"],"answer":"gcd = 12"},{"t":"p","text":"lcm(a, b) = ab / gcd(a, b)."}]},{"say":"Modular arithmetic is \"clock math\": numbers wrap around after reaching the modulus.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"a ≡ b (mod n) means n divides a − b, or equivalently a and b have the same remainder when divided by n."},{"t":"p","text":"You can add, subtract and multiply in mod n and reduce at any step: (a · b) mod n = ((a mod n)(b mod n)) mod n."},{"t":"ex","problem":"What is 7¹⁰⁰ mod 6?","steps":["7 ≡ 1 (mod 6)","1¹⁰⁰ = 1"],"answer":"1"},{"t":"p","text":"Applications: checking digits (ISBNs, credit cards), hashing, and RSA encryption, which relies on the difficulty of factoring large numbers."}]}],[{"say":"Counting cleverly is a superpower. It lets you count huge numbers of possibilities without listing them.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Product rule: if a task has m ways then n ways, there are m·n ways. Sum rule: if a task can be done in m OR n ways (no overlap), there are m + n ways."},{"t":"p","text":"Permutations (order matters): P(n, r) = n!/(n − r)!. Combinations (order doesn't matter): C(n, r) = n!/(r!(n − r)!)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"How many 5-card hands come from a 52-card deck?","steps":["Order doesn't matter: C(52, 5)"],"answer":"2,598,960"},{"t":"p","text":"The pigeonhole principle: if n + 1 objects go into n boxes, some box gets at least two."}]},{"say":"With counting skills, you can compute probabilities exactly.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"For equally likely outcomes, P(E) = |E| / |S|, where S is the sample space."},{"t":"p","text":"Rules: P(not E) = 1 − P(E); for independent events P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B); conditional P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B)."},{"t":"p","text":"Expected value E[X] = Σ x·P(x) gives the long-run average outcome."},{"t":"ex","problem":"You win $10 if you roll a 6 and pay $1 otherwise. What's the expected value?","steps":["10·(1/6) + (−1)·(5/6) = 10/6 − 5/6"],"answer":"about $0.83 per game"},{"t":"p","text":"Bayes' theorem updates probabilities with new evidence: P(A | B) = P(B | A)P(A)/P(B)."}]}],[{"say":"Graphs model networks: friendships, roads, the internet. They're everywhere in computer science.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A graph has vertices (nodes) connected by edges. The degree of a vertex is its number of edges."},{"t":"key","text":"Handshake theorem: the sum of all vertex degrees equals twice the number of edges, so the number of odd-degree vertices is always even."},{"t":"list","items":["A path is a sequence of edges; a circuit is a path that returns to its start.","An Euler circuit uses every edge exactly once; it exists in a connected graph exactly when every vertex has even degree.","A Hamilton circuit visits every vertex exactly once."]},{"t":"p","text":"Directed graphs have edges with directions; weighted graphs have costs on edges (shortest-path algorithms like Dijkstra's use them)."}]},{"say":"Trees are graphs with no cycles. They organize data in file systems, family trees and search algorithms.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A tree is a connected graph with no cycles. A tree with n vertices has exactly n − 1 edges."},{"t":"p","text":"A rooted tree has a designated root; vertices have parents and children, and vertices with no children are leaves. In a binary tree, each vertex has at most two children."},{"t":"p","text":"Binary search trees store data so searches can skip half the remaining data at each step. Traversals visit every vertex: preorder, inorder and postorder."},{"t":"p","text":"A spanning tree connects all vertices of a graph using a subset of its edges. Minimum spanning trees (Kruskal's or Prim's algorithm) connect everything at the lowest total cost."}]}]];
export default text;
